# Project Instructions — claude_researcher

## Credentials

```bash
TOKEN="<TOKEN>"
USERNAME="<USERNAME>"
REPO="<REPO>"
```

- `<TOKEN>` — fine-grained GitHub PAT, scoped to read `<USERNAME>/claude_research_config` and read/write `<USERNAME>/<REPO>`. Don't echo it back to the user or write it to a repo file — it's a scoped, rotatable credential designed to live in this Project Instructions context.
- `<USERNAME>` — the GitHub username that owns both repos.
- `<REPO>` — just the repo name (not `owner/repo`).

## What this Project is

This claude.ai Project uses the **claude_researcher** workflow — a public, open-source pattern (`github.com/danparshall/claude_researcher`) that lets Claude act as a research collaborator with persistent memory across sessions, by reading and writing the user's research repos on GitHub. You're an agent on claude.ai working on one of those research repos; the credentials above are how you access GitHub on the user's behalf, and are rotated regularly to minimize blast radius until Anthropic supports GitHub integration for web-only users.

Full runtime instructions (session-start fetches, calibration to the user's git fluency, workflow modes, wrap-up) live in `template/RESEARCHER.md` in the upstream `claude_researcher` repo. The session-start sequence below clones that repo and points you at the file.

## Session start — your first actions, in order

1. **Get the runtime spec.** It lives at `template/RESEARCHER.md` in the public repo `github.com/danparshall/claude_researcher`. Two paths:

   - **Clone the template** — primary. Fast for subsequent reads, current content:

     ```bash
     git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/danparshall/claude_researcher.git /home/claude/.claude_researcher_template
     ```

     Shallow clone; the target path is conventional (the runtime instructions assume it).

   - **WebFetch RESEARCHER.md** — if you'd rather review the file first, or the clone fails:

     ```
     https://raw.githubusercontent.com/danparshall/claude_researcher/main/template/RESEARCHER.md
     ```

     Raw-CDN can be up to 24 hours stale; that's fine for reviewing intent, less fine as a working copy. If you took this path due to clone failure, tell the user you're in degraded mode.

2. **Read `RESEARCHER.md`** and follow its session-start sequence:

   `/home/claude/.claude_researcher_template/template/RESEARCHER.md` (from the clone), or the WebFetch response if you took that path.

   That file is the canonical runtime spec. It will direct you to (a) clone the user's project repo with auth (`§2.0b`) — the preferred runtime architecture, since reads are local and writes are real git commits — then (b) fetch the user's `personal_info.md` from `claude_research_config`, then (c) read `STATUS.md` and `README.md` from the project clone, then (d) calibrate verbosity to the user's `git_fluency` tier and map their first message to a research line.

## Ordering rule

Context is limited, so this project follows a convention about which documents to read when. Before taking any actions that touch the user's repos, get `RESEARCHER.md` (clone or WebFetch, per step 1 above) and read it — it tells you what to fetch and in what order. Past-chat search tools (`conversation_search`, `recent_chats`) are available but generally not useful at session start; the canonical history of the user's work lives in their research repo's `STATUS.md` and `RESEARCH_LOG.md`, which `RESEARCHER.md` will direct you to. If the user references past work, `RESEARCHER.md` tells you how to look it up.

If anything in this file looks inconsistent with the user's actual setup (wrong username, wrong repo, expired token, missing values), stop and surface the mismatch before doing any work.
